  chemistryPair('cells','formula','medium',['Which equation gives the standard cell emf from standard reduction potentials?','E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode',['E°cell = E°cathode + E°anode in every case','E°cell = nF','E°cell = ΔG°/RT'],['Both listed electrode potentials use the reduction convention.','Reverse the anode process, so its reduction potential is subtracted.']],['Which relation connects Gibbs energy and cell emf?','ΔG = −nFE',['ΔG = nF/E','ΔG = nE/F','ΔG = +nFE in every case'],['Electrical work from a reversible cell is related to transferred charge nF.','The thermodynamic sign convention gives ΔG = −nFE.']]),
  chemistryPair('conductance','numerical','medium',['A conductor has resistance 50 Ω. Find its conductance.','0.020 S',['50 S','2 S','0.002 S'],['Conductance is the reciprocal of resistance.','G = 1/50 = 0.020 S.']],['A conductor has resistance 200 Ω. Find its conductance.','0.005 S',['200 S','0.05 S','5 S'],['Use G = 1/R.','The reciprocal of 200 Ω is 0.005 S.']]),
  chemistryPair('cells','application','hard',['At equilibrium, the emf of a reversible cell for the reaction is:','Zero',['Always 1 V','Always negative','Infinite'],['At equilibrium, ΔG = 0.','Since ΔG = −nFE and nF is nonzero, E must be zero.']],['For a spontaneous galvanic-cell reaction under the specified conditions, which sign pair is correct?','E > 0 and ΔG < 0',['E < 0 and ΔG < 0','E > 0 and ΔG > 0','E = 0 and ΔG > 0'],['Spontaneity requires negative ΔG.','The relation ΔG = −nFE therefore requires positive cell emf.']])
