  chemistryPair('alkenes','application','hard',['Reductive ozonolysis of but-2-ene produces:','Two molecules of ethanal per alkene molecule',['Only propanone','Methanal and propanone','Two molecules of methanal'],['Split the C=C bond and convert each alkene carbon to a carbonyl carbon.','Both sides of CH₃CH=CHCH₃ become CH₃CHO.']],['Reductive ozonolysis of 2-methylpropene produces:','Propanone and methanal',['Two molecules of ethanal','Only ethanoic acid','Propanal and hydrogen'],['Cleaving (CH₃)₂C=CH₂ creates two carbonyl groups.','The substituted carbon gives propanone and the terminal CH₂ gives methanal.']]),
  chemistryPair('alkenes','misconception','medium',['The usual peroxide-induced anti-Markovnikov addition applies to:','HBr',['HCl','HI','HF'],['A viable radical chain requires favourable propagation steps.','The common peroxide effect for hydrogen halides is observed with HBr.']],['Why does propene normally give the Markovnikov product on electrophilic addition of HBr without peroxides?','The pathway forms the more stable carbocation',['A primary carbocation is always most stable','Bromide attacks a neutron','The double bond never reacts'],['Initial proton addition can form alternative carbocations.','Formation of the more stable secondary carbocation favours the usual orientation.']]),
  chemistryPair('alkanes','mixed','medium',['Which conformation of ethane has lower torsional energy?','Staggered',['Eclipsed','Both always have identical energy','A planar carbon skeleton with no C–H bonds'],['Staggering separates the C–H bonds on adjacent carbons.','This reduces torsional strain relative to the eclipsed arrangement.']],['Which conformation of ethane has greater torsional strain?','Eclipsed',['Staggered','Every possible conformation equally','A structure with no rotation axis'],['In an eclipsed conformation, adjacent C–H bonds line up.','This gives greater torsional strain than the staggered arrangement.']]),
  chemistryPair('overview','beginner','easy',['Which hydrocarbon contains a carbon–carbon triple bond?','Propyne',['Propane','Propene','Cyclopropane'],['The ending -yne indicates a triple bond.','Propyne contains a C≡C bond.']],['Which hydrocarbon is saturated?','Butane',['But-1-ene','But-2-yne','Benzene'],['A saturated hydrocarbon has no carbon–carbon multiple bond.','Butane has only single bonds.']]),
  chemistryPair('overview','formula','medium',['The general formula of an acyclic monoalkene is:','C_nH_(2n)',['C_nH_(2n+2)','C_nH_(2n−2)','C_nH_(2n+4)'],['Relative to an alkane, one double bond reduces hydrogen count by 2.','The resulting formula is C_nH_(2n).']],['The general formula of an acyclic monoalkyne is:','C_nH_(2n−2)',['C_nH_(2n)','C_nH_(2n+2)','C_nH_(2n+4)'],['A triple bond reduces hydrogen count by 4 relative to the corresponding alkane.','The formula becomes C_nH_(2n−2).']]),
  chemistryPair('overview','numerical','medium',['How many moles of O₂ are needed for complete combustion of 1 mol propane, C₃H₈?',5,[3,4,8],['Balance carbon and hydrogen to get 3CO₂ and 4H₂O.','These products contain 10 oxygen atoms, requiring 5O₂.']],['How many moles of O₂ are needed for complete combustion of 1 mol methane, CH₄?',2,[1,3,4],['The balanced products are CO₂ and 2H₂O.','They need four oxygen atoms, supplied by 2O₂.']]),
  chemistryPair('aromatics','application','hard',['The electrophile in the usual nitration of benzene is:','NO₂⁺',['NO₂⁻','NH₄⁺','OH⁻'],['Concentrated acids generate the nitronium ion.','NO₂⁺ attacks the aromatic ring in electrophilic substitution.']],['An activating hydroxyl substituent on benzene generally directs further electrophilic substitution to:','Ortho and para positions',['Only meta positions','Only the carbon bearing OH','No position in any reaction'],['The oxygen lone pair can donate into the ring by resonance.','This enhances electron density at ortho and para positions.']])
