  chemistryPair('reactions','numerical','medium',['How many constitutional isomers have molecular formula C₃H₇Br?',2,[1,3,4],['Bromine may attach to a terminal or the middle carbon of propane.','These are 1-bromopropane and 2-bromopropane.']],['How many constitutional isomers have molecular formula C₄H₉Br?',4,[2,3,5],['The butane skeleton permits 1- and 2-bromo substitution.','The 2-methylpropane skeleton permits two further distinct bromides.','The total is four constitutional isomers.']]),
  chemistryPair('reactions','application','hard',['An SN2 reaction at a stereogenic carbon generally produces:','Inversion of configuration at that centre',['Retention in every case','A planar free carbocation first','No bond cleavage'],['Backside attack occurs opposite the leaving group.','The tetrahedral arrangement inverts during substitution.']],['Why is chlorobenzene less reactive than chloroethane in ordinary SN1/SN2 conditions?','Its aryl C–Cl bond and electronic structure resist those pathways',['It has no C–Cl bond','Its carbon is a free carbocation','Chlorine has no electrons'],['Resonance gives the aryl C–Cl bond partial double-bond character.','A phenyl carbocation is unfavourable, and ordinary backside SN2 attack at the aryl carbon is difficult.']]),
  chemistryPair('reactions','misconception','easy',['Which statement about the C–Cl bond in a typical alkyl chloride is correct?','It is polarized toward chlorine',['It is polarized toward carbon because chlorine is less electronegative','It contains two neutrons instead of electrons','It is always nonpolar'],['Chlorine is more electronegative than carbon.','The bond has Cδ⁺–Clδ⁻ polarization.']],['A nucleophile in substitution attacks mainly the:','Electron-deficient carbon bonded to the leaving group',['Leaving-group nucleus only','Nearest neutron','Solvent container'],['The carbon bonded to a halogen is often electrophilic.','The nucleophile donates an electron pair to that carbon.']]),
  chemistryPair('environment','mixed','medium',['Chloroform exposed to air and light can form which highly toxic product?','Phosgene, COCl₂',['Glucose','Ethanol','Ammonia'],['Oxidation of chloroform can occur under unsuitable storage conditions.','The resulting carbonyl chloride, phosgene, is highly toxic.']],['The antiseptic once associated with a characteristic yellow solid and smell is:','Iodoform',['Methane','Ethanol vapour','Sodium chloride'],['Iodoform has formula CHI₃ and is a yellow solid.','It has been used as an antiseptic material.']]),
  chemistryPair('reactions','beginner','easy',['A carbon bonded to halogen and to only one other carbon is classified as:','Primary',['Secondary','Tertiary','Quaternary'],['Classify the carbon bearing the halogen.','One attached carbon makes that centre primary.']],['A carbon bonded to halogen and to three other carbons is classified as:','Tertiary',['Primary','Secondary','Methyl'],['Count the carbon groups attached to the halogen-bearing carbon.','Three carbon groups make it tertiary.']]),
  chemistryPair('reactions','formula','medium',['The characteristic rate law for a simple SN1 process is:','Rate = k[RX]',['Rate = k[RX][Nu⁻]','Rate = k/[RX]','Rate = k[Nu⁻]²'],['The slow step is unimolecular ionization of the substrate.','Its rate depends on substrate concentration.']],['The characteristic rate law for a simple SN2 process is:','Rate = k[RX][Nu⁻]',['Rate = k[RX] only','Rate = k/[Nu⁻]','Rate = k[RX]² only'],['Substrate and nucleophile participate in the single substitution step.','The rate is proportional to both concentrations.']]),
  chemistryPair('reactions','numerical','medium',['How many configurational stereoisomers does 2-bromobutane have?',2,[1,3,4],['Carbon 2 is attached to H, Br, CH₃ and C₂H₅.','One stereogenic centre gives a pair of enantiomers.']],['How many stereogenic centres are in 2-bromobutane?',1,[0,2,3],['Check each carbon for four different substituents.','Only carbon 2 satisfies that condition.']]),
  chemistryPair('reactions','application','hard',['A tertiary alkyl bromide reacts in a polar protic medium by an SN1 pathway. The slow step is generally:','Ionization to a carbocation and bromide',['Backside attack before any ionization','Formation of a triple bond in every case','Nuclear rearrangement'],['SN1 proceeds through a carbocation intermediate.','Formation of that intermediate by C–Br cleavage is the rate-determining step.']],['Which substitution pathway is usually favoured by a strong nucleophile attacking a minimally hindered primary substrate?','SN2',['SN1 through a stable primary carbocation','Only radical substitution','Only aromatic electrophilic substitution'],['Low steric hindrance favours backside attack.','A strong nucleophile can participate directly in the bimolecular SN2 step.']])
